#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441533} :activity/id #uuid "fa9d4c91-2ac2-50a9-bf17-b5e5e2265182" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049830 :topic/id #uuid "1725b401-08f0-5bd3-9105-3249e65bebf9" :topic/math-academy-id 3211} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "455a4520-a40d-5887-99e6-b09e912bfc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312537} {:answer/id #uuid "5879445a-49b1-58fc-a9ed-b1741a769ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312684} {:answer/id #uuid "d629babc-72e0-5d25-b2dc-6cb329f388fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313662} {:answer/id #uuid "fb999ea2-3848-5b11-affc-1a7459bb1972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313662} :answer-field/id #uuid "48394d30-18eb-527e-b122-4aec3e7da5f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "449d7389-bdf6-56cd-b81e-df09c963917f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312529} {:answer/id #uuid "8830110d-9399-5eb7-b924-9838af2e633d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313062} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7f3c32-7c3f-5498-95a8-bb7c079184b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313206} {:answer/id #uuid "e477f342-1f36-504e-b43b-ceb0045b9441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313779} :answer-field/id #uuid "64cb7ecf-ae6c-55b0-bbd8-49577bd75c09" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1cf1150e-7ecc-59c2-a214-35eca3b99cbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338915" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in {N}_{0}.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 3\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n6\\sqrt{\\lambda }=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(1+2n)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in {N}_{0}.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{144}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1cab76e5-6c1e-514f-a717-d9c42a4499b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66f6bef1-778e-54ac-8bca-389bd469b61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391711} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8245c8-0e8a-5ec6-812c-40fb520a0dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{400}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391712} {:answer/id #uuid "8091ad43-a6d6-542a-9238-3300819d2ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391713} {:answer/id #uuid "dae1120f-db9d-59df-ac9c-c5067e0542fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391712} :answer-field/id #uuid "77dfda2b-2332-5cb1-bf2d-c957e336718a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d90220b1-6fc2-5f68-a111-69d4ae63b772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391716} {:answer/id #uuid "0973d359-8928-55e0-aa49-7281d37dd42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391717} {:answer/id #uuid "b7595082-877b-5b1f-81f8-341af224951c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391718} {:answer/id #uuid "137f89ad-4c21-5fc6-a296-ef6fbb06f150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391719}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391718} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8c60513-d952-5f41-b9e9-c5398ae1b7aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "72f2d583-8ffc-50a1-bdb5-e2d5a11cd167" :question/math-academy-id "q-338916" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)=y(5)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 5,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(5)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(5)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n20\\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{400}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a1cfa99-80c5-51a8-869c-32eec8bcaf70"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7708b0c3-fc6f-5c9e-b153-91f22444d86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441522} {:answer/id #uuid "858b6a2d-f127-50fb-b974-fcb22a273b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-2x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441523} {:answer/id #uuid "ad35330a-43cf-5531-8b67-e32f2b60d443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441525} {:answer/id #uuid "b965e478-138a-5a0a-89f2-8c72e03c9a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441526} {:answer/id #uuid "e321edcb-caed-5e92-a02b-54c8f1f87eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441522} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2436d5-f9a4-5374-af03-fa25b7418d63" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164797} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf795f77-40cd-5766-b8c9-f78e51fb5b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164804} {:answer/id #uuid "541797df-f259-5d14-9648-fec3c5056a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164806} {:answer/id #uuid "7816f9cc-e5dd-5a2b-b9db-bd12a1bacd3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164808} {:answer/id #uuid "a1488815-d1a6-5503-a380-473f0f85b5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441524} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d41e3c-6c2e-5718-8ed2-cbf24e5e3949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186164806} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fba1ed3-a3ce-5014-baab-3fc389f47995" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "868ac7c6-b3e9-504e-8014-26f63ba38d5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338722" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8\\lambda {y}^{′}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-8\\lambda m+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-4\\lambda )(m-4\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=4\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{4\\lambda x}+4B\\lambda x{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B{e}^{4\\lambda }+B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{4\\lambda }+2B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{4\\lambda }(1+2\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{4\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f1e042a8-1faa-5865-8d30-e1ea1fe82b24" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441532}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d902eda-8b02-59d9-817f-5548cb8fa47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{2x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312196} {:answer/id #uuid "626a0064-27a3-570a-9c3a-b1e55d495db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312759} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd9d1e5-b8dd-5125-8738-bdcfe733d92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312855} {:answer/id #uuid "784800d0-17ce-5cef-9605-5d1cd6a346f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-3x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312759} :answer-field/id #uuid "c06aeca6-ffb9-5370-9b57-f3f69e5fd517" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208030f7-aa5b-5fe3-a831-ca0b389d652b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312220} {:answer/id #uuid "9095fcf6-3fc8-53ea-a71e-67a00e1fa891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313128} {:answer/id #uuid "999f7576-c8ca-5d99-bba8-73458fe88f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{8}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313191} {:answer/id #uuid "ef630254-396a-5be5-81c2-167737583600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312220} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ce9ea6-e543-5afd-84eb-289c55556054" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "faaf0705-3b7e-515b-ad1e-e97ff79382c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338729" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8\\lambda {y}^{′}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-8\\lambda m+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-4\\lambda )(m-4\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=4\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda x}+B{e}^{4\\lambda x}+4B\\lambda x{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda }+B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4A\\lambda +B+4B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+2B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(4A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(4A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+8A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+(4+16\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda  & 1+4\\lambda  \\\\ 1+8\\lambda  & 4(1+4\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+4\\lambda )-(1+4\\lambda )(1+8\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -(1+8\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -1-8\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )(8\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"} :step/id #uuid "f8b73451-fda1-5086-8156-fc15d81c7a88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eaa833a-4503-548a-adc1-bc8b70b6166d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312205} {:answer/id #uuid "279bdd25-77a2-5011-9957-def30851df49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312278} {:answer/id #uuid "72a1092b-3982-5491-8c11-dc1a005977e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312878} {:answer/id #uuid "aa46fa52-4421-518d-9981-7466aa259742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313310} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b022b8-6af6-5df9-be9e-a798772ed840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313365} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2edbe2-e5fc-501d-8338-f82c039d22d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313310} :answer-field/id #uuid "66ab1828-984f-5595-87cc-c82810d14ddb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314298} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5ba48c-fe59-549e-9f9d-bccf40a0107c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312060} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb4fd22-8b2c-5ec6-805e-dce5366cca24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312343} {:answer/id #uuid "88a0374c-7edd-59be-afc7-c18de6554e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313065} {:answer/id #uuid "98c8320c-12e9-5482-a30d-31b246374d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313187} {:answer/id #uuid "a989bed3-15be-5630-9343-79e34351b318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313305}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313065} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9e367a-b19a-558c-ac99-d9fc647d67bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9fd0718d-5c1d-56bf-aa5a-76fcf93883d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338667" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\lambda =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fa5303a7-7edf-519b-8a8a-d599ebe59258" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441531}}] :activity/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441521}]}
